\section{Literature and context check}
The original report, p. 3012, explicitly gives the rank-one sorting observation and asks about rank two and beyond. The representation of the real weight matrix is not fixed. The attempt separates an entry-query obstruction from a concrete failure of extending one-coordinate sorting to factored rank two.
\section{Research attempt: A rank-one access obstruction and a rank-two sorting failure}
In a deterministic entry-query model, an unknown rank-one matrix need not reveal its factors quickly. Consider nonnegative matrices with exactly one positive entry and all others zero; every such matrix has rank exactly one. Suppose an algorithm has received zero on $q<n^2-n$ queried locations and now outputs a perfect matching $P$. More than $n$ locations are unqueried, while $P$ contains exactly $n$ locations. Choose an unqueried location outside $P$ and put a one there, zero elsewhere. This matrix is compatible with every query response, has rank one, gives the reported matching weight zero, and has optimum one because the special edge can be extended to a perfect matching. Hence no exact algorithm can always terminate with a correct matching after fewer than $n^2-n$ queries on this adversarial transcript. In particular the requested near-linear rank-one bound cannot hold for this unrestricted entry-query representation.

This is not an obstruction when factors are supplied. If $W=uv^T$, sort both vectors in increasing order and match corresponding entries. Any assignment with $u_i\le u_j$ but assigned $v_a>v_b$ improves or stays equal after swapping, since the gain is $(u_j-u_i)(v_a-v_b)\ge0$. Repeated removal of inversions yields the sorted matching, proving optimality even with negative entries and ties. Factor access permits the report's $O(n\log n)$ comparison bound.

A straightforward rank-two extension, sorting points by their first coordinate, fails. Use row factors $u_1=(0,M)$, $u_2=(1,0)$ and column factors $v_1=(0,1)$, $v_2=(1,M)$, with $M\ge2$. Both first-coordinate orders are $1,2$, so that rule picks the diagonal of
\[
W=\begin{pmatrix}M&M^2\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Its value is $M+1$, while the other matching has value $M^2>M+1$ for $M\ge2$. The determinant is $M\ne0$, so this is genuinely rank two. The difference in values can grow quadratically in $M$. This refutes that fixed-coordinate rule, not all geometric rank-two algorithms or all possible projections chosen adaptively from the data.
\section{Verification and adversarial audit}
The query adversary uses matrices of exact rank one, avoiding a zero-matrix promise violation. A positive singleton edge always extends to a full matching in the complete bipartite graph. The rank-two determinant and both matching values are explicit; sorting ties are not involved in the first coordinates.
\paragraph{Audit corrections.} The access model is explicit, and the adversarial transcript is used only while compatible with the exact-rank promise. A failed coordinate sort is not marketed as a lower bound for every rank-two algorithm.
\paragraph{External computation.} None; symbolic arguments and boundary checks only.
\section{Scope and remaining gaps}
The original target is representation-sensitive. An entry-query version has the proved obstruction, but the factored low-rank version intended by the rank-one observation remains unresolved beyond the demonstrated failed sorting rule. No full negative answer under all input models is claimed.
\paragraph{Integration review.} Codex (GPT-6; exact serving revision not exposed) reviewed the written arguments and expanded layout and source-reference wrappers. Repository metadata and the production web renderer were checked using Python 3.13 and JavaScript with MathJax 3.2.2. This model-assisted review is not independent human proof verification; no PDF compilation or formal proof checking is asserted.

